It's that magic moment when the product essentially summons itself because it's welded

10
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.900
directly to a user's internal emotion. Maybe it's boredom, or in Mahal's case, it was that

11
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.650
creeping morning anxiety about disorganized tasks. Let's break down the psychological framework that

12
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:33.330
makes this all happen. Section one, the four-step habit loop, engineering retention from the inside

13
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:38.900
out. This brilliantly illustrates what's called the hook model. It's a self-reinforcing cycle

14
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.820
that drives retention, lowers your acquisition costs, and dramatically pushes up customer lifetime

15
00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:50.180
value. It breaks down into four steps. First, the trigger, that's the emotional spark. Second is the

16
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:55.620
action a radically simple behavior third you have the variable reward which is an unpredictable

17
00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.740
payoff and finally investment which is the user effort that automatically loads the next cycle

18
00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:06.550
now it's super interesting to see exactly how mahal translated those four abstract steps

19
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:12.230
